# Setting and conventions {#sec:setting}

**Punchline.** The commuting family is the set of Laurent coefficients of a column-ordered quantum determinant; on any state it is described by six numbers $(H_s,a_s,b_s)_{s=1,2}$, with a scalar double pole $c_2=\lambda_2(\lambda_1+1)$ and no triple pole. The bra vacua are $e_1^T\rho(n)$ with explicit Gauss parameters, and they live in a frame tied to the diagonal frame of the eigenstates by an explicit scalar $\kappa^G$.

## The model and its Hamiltonians {#sec:model}

We work with $\mathfrak{gl}_3$ and a two-site chain; both sites carry the same irreducible representation $V_\Lambda$, $\Lambda=(\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\lambda_3)$ with $\lambda_3=0$ throughout.^[All representations are written in the Gelfand--Tsetlin (GT) basis with plain, non-Hermitian rational generators $E_{ij}$ and the invariant diagonal metric $G$ ($E_{ij}^TG=GE_{ji}$); this is the convention of `Paul/Python/gl3_reps.py` and of the notebooks' `JJ`. Raising operators are $E_{ij}$ with $i<j$; $e_1$ is the highest-weight ket and covectors are rows acted on from the right, so $e_1^TE_{ij}\neq0$ only for $i\le j$. Sources: `Gaudin-sl3-CG-plan.md` section 1; `RESULTS-Qside.md` section 0.] On site $s$ the generators are $E^{(1)}=E\otimes1$, $E^{(2)}=1\otimes E$, they obey $[E_{ij},E_{kl}]=\delta_{jk}E_{il}-\delta_{li}E_{kj}$, and the inhomogeneities are $\theta=(\theta_1,\theta_2)$, $\theta_{12}=\theta_1-\theta_2$. There is no $h$ anywhere in the Gaudin model. The twist is a Lie-algebra element $g=\mathrm{diag}(\zeta_1,\zeta_2,\zeta_3)$ and the Lax matrix is
$$
L_g(u)_{ij}=\zeta_i\,\delta_{ij}+\sum_{s=1}^2\frac{E^{(s)}_{ij}}{u-\theta_s}.\label{eq:Lax}
$$
The commuting family is generated by the column-ordered quantum determinant of $\partial_u-L_g(u)$, with the derivative moved to the right ($\partial f=f\partial+f'$):
$$
\mathrm{cdet}(\partial_u-L_g)=\sum_{\sigma\in S_3}\mathrm{sgn}\,\sigma\; M_{\sigma(1)1}M_{\sigma(2)2}M_{\sigma(3)3}=\partial^3-S_1(u)\,\partial^2+S_2(u)\,\partial-S_3(u),\qquad M=\partial_u\mathbf 1-L_g.\label{eq:cdet}
$$
Writing $l_{ij}=L_g(u)_{ij}$ and $l'_{ij}=\partial_ul_{ij}$, $l''_{ij}=\partial_u^2l_{ij}$, and keeping operator products in the order written, the coefficients are
$$
\begin{aligned}
S_1&=l_{11}+l_{22}+l_{33},\\
S_2&=\sum_{i<j}\bigl(l_{ii}l_{jj}-l_{ji}l_{ij}\bigr)-l'_{22}-2\,l'_{33},\\
S_3&=l''_{33}-l_{11}l'_{33}-l_{22}l'_{33}-l'_{22}l_{33}+l_{31}l'_{13}+l_{32}l'_{23}+l'_{32}l_{23}\\
&\quad+l_{11}l_{22}l_{33}-l_{11}l_{32}l_{23}-l_{21}l_{12}l_{33}+l_{21}l_{32}l_{13}+l_{31}l_{12}l_{23}-l_{31}l_{22}l_{13}.
\end{aligned}\label{eq:S123}
$$
We checked that $[S_a(u),S_b(v)]=0$ exactly (exact rationals, 3$\times$3 grids of rational $u,v$, for $[2,1,0]$, $[3,1,0]$, $[3,2,0]$), that $S_1$ is a scalar and that the weights are conserved.^[Agents W1 (`Gaudin-L2-chain.wb`, summary cell), P1 (`RESULTS-Qside.md` section 0), P3, P4. The convention was not tuned: it worked at the first attempt. The row-ordered product with $E_{ij}$ and the column-ordered product with $E_{ji}$ do \emph{not} commute (274 non-zero entries in $[S_2,S_3]$); the doubly flipped variant is just the transposed chain.]

## Laurent data {#sec:laurent}

Every non-scalar information about a state is contained in the Laurent coefficients at the two sites:
$$
\begin{aligned}
S_1&=\sigma+|\Lambda|\sum_s\frac1{u-\theta_s},\qquad \sigma=\zeta_1+\zeta_2+\zeta_3,\\
S_2&=e_2(\zeta)+\sum_s\Bigl[\frac{H_s}{u-\theta_s}+\frac{c_2}{(u-\theta_s)^2}\Bigr],\\
S_3&=e_3(\zeta)+\sum_s\Bigl[\frac{b_s}{u-\theta_s}+\frac{a_s}{(u-\theta_s)^2}\Bigr],
\end{aligned}\label{eq:laurent}
$$
where $e_k(\zeta)$ are elementary symmetric polynomials, $|\Lambda|=\lambda_1+\lambda_2+\lambda_3$, and
$$
c_2=\lambda_2(\lambda_1+1)\label{eq:c2}
$$
is a scalar. $S_3$ has no triple pole. On an eigenstate the six operators $H_1,H_2,a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2$ become six numbers; this is all the state-dependent data that the Gaudin Hamiltonians carry. They are not independent: three no-log conditions per site (Section~\ref{sec:nolog}) cut the solution set down to a finite one.

The value $c_2=\lambda_2(\lambda_1+1)$ deserves a remark, because we got it wrong twice. It equals $|\Lambda|$ for $[2,1,0]$ and $[3,1,0]$, so those two representations cannot tell the two apart; the first guess, half the quadratic Casimir, gives the right $3$ for $[2,1,0]$ but $6$ for $[3,1,0]$, whose true value is $4$. The scan over representations is in Table~\ref{tab:c2}. For one-row representations ($\lambda_2=0$) the double pole is the scalar $0$.^[Agent P3 (`RESULTS-Qside-310.md` section 0) found $c_2=\lambda_2(\lambda_1+1)$ on $(1,1,0),(2,1,0),(3,1,0),(2,2,0),(3,2,0)$ and reported the double pole as "not scalar" for $(n,0,0)$; agent G1 (`RESULTS-general-kernel.md` K2) later checked $[1,0,0],[2,0,0],[3,0,0]$ on $9/36/100$ states and found the scalar $0$ every time. We take G1's statement, as the later round. $(2,1,1)$ has $c_2=8$ but an $S_3$ triple pole and was not used.]

