The first Laurent coefficient has an explicit form, and its sign cost us a round-1 correction. With $C_\zeta^{(s)}=\sum_i\zeta_iE^{(s)}_{ii}$ and $W=\sum_k\zeta_k(E^{(1)}+E^{(2)})_{kk}$,
$$
H_1=-\Bigl[\sum_i\zeta_iE^{(1)}_{ii}+\frac1{\theta_{12}}\sum_{i,k}E^{(1)}_{ik}E^{(2)}_{ki}\Bigr]+|\Lambda|\sigma+\frac{|\Lambda|^2}{\theta_{12}},\qquad H_1+H_2=-W+2|\Lambda|\sigma,\label{eq:H1}
$$
and $H_2$ is the same with $1\leftrightarrow2$ and $\theta_{12}\to-\theta_{12}$. The sign is $-$ in front of the bracket, not $+$ as the first plan had it: it follows from the residue at $\theta_1$ of $\sum_{i<j}(l_{ii}l_{jj}-l_{ji}l_{ij})$.^[Agent W1, `Gaudin-L2-chain.wb` summary cell ("Sign difference to the plan"), exact on $[2,1,0]$; agents P3 and P4 report the same explicit form for $[3,1,0]$ and $[3,2,0]$ (exact fits, 0 mismatches). The plan `Gaudin-sl3-CG-plan.md` section 1 carries the corrected row.] The same formula at $\zeta=0$ gives $H_1=\bigl(|\Lambda|^2-\tfrac12[C_2(\mu)-2C_2(\Lambda)]\bigr)/\theta_{12}$ on a state of total weight $\mu$ (Section~\ref{sec:casimir}).

## The dictionary to XXX {#sec:dict}

The Gaudin family is the limit of the XXX transfer matrices at twist $z=e^{h\zeta}$. Expanding $t_1(u)/\nu(u)=3+hA_1+h^2A_2+h^3A_3+\dots$ and $t_2(u)/(\nu(u)\nu(u-h))=3+hB_1+h^2B_2+\dots$ with $\nu(u)=\Pi(u)=(u-\theta_1)(u-\theta_2)$ one finds, exactly,
$$
\begin{aligned}
&A_1=S_1,\quad B_1=2S_1,\quad B_2=A_2+\tfrac12S_1^2+\tfrac12S_1',\\
&S_2=-A_2+\tfrac12S_1^2+\tfrac12S_1',\quad S_3=A_3-B_3+\tfrac16S_1^3+\tfrac12S_1S_1'+\tfrac12S_1'',
\end{aligned}\label{eq:dict}
$$
with $S_k(u\to\infty)=e_k(\zeta)$. The relations $A_1=S_1$ and $B_1=2S_1$ are forced by the Baxter equations at orders $h^1$ and $h^2$; nothing in the dictionary needs more than $S_1$ and its derivatives.^[Agent P2, `RESULTS-xxx-limit.md` section X2: exact rational series in $h$, checked at four values of $u$ and three twist sets, and at finite $h$ to $O(h^3)$ residuals. P2 also found the vacuum, frame and Baxter-to-oper statements used below.] This is why the cdet convention could be pinned independently of the chain-side commutation test: the plan's convention is the only one that fits.

## Frames, vacua and the reference covector {#sec:frames}

Eigenstates $\Psi_B$ of the diagonal-twist Hamiltonians live in what we call the \emph{diagonal frame}. The SoV construction naturally lives in the \emph{companion frame}, where the twist is replaced by its Lie-algebra companion matrix, built from $e_k=e_k(\zeta)$,
$$
g_c(\zeta)=\begin{pmatrix}e_1&-e_2&e_3\\1&0&0\\0&1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad P_{\rm aux}(\zeta)\ \text{with rows}\ (1,\ \zeta_i-e_1,\ \zeta_i^2-e_1\zeta_i+e_2),\label{eq:gc}
$$
so that $P_{\rm aux}g_cP_{\rm aux}^{-1}=\mathrm{diag}(\zeta)$. The physical-space image of the frame change is $P^G_{\rm phys}(\zeta)=\rho\bigl((P_{\rm aux}(\zeta)^{-1})^T\bigr)$, and the reference covector is $x_0^G=x(\zeta)\otimes x(\zeta)$ with $x(\zeta)=e_1^T\bigl(P^G_{\rm phys}\bigr)^{-1}$ (one site). For the adjoint, in notebook order,
$$
x(\zeta)=\bigl(\zeta_2-\zeta_1,\ \zeta_3-\zeta_1,\ \zeta_2-\zeta_1,\ 2\zeta_3-\zeta_1-\zeta_2,\ 3(\zeta_2-\zeta_1),\ 3(\zeta_3-\zeta_1),\ 2(\zeta_3-\zeta_2),\ 3(\zeta_3-\zeta_2)\bigr),\label{eq:x0}
$$
a covector whose components are homogeneous of degree $\lambda_2$ in $\zeta$ for every representation we have looked at.^[Exactly linear: agent D2, `RESULTS-twovac-limit.md` L1(b), with the XXX $x_0(z)$ exactly linear in $z$ at every order in $h$; $x_0(e^{h\zeta})=h\,x(\zeta)+O(h^2)$. Degree $\lambda_2$ in general: agent W4, `Gaudin-untwisted-exact.wb` E5 and the `XVEC` records of the data files ($[1,1,0]$: $(z_2-z_1,z_3-z_1,z_3-z_2)$; $[2,2,0]$: quadratic).] We expected $x_0^G$ not to be parallel to the $h\to0$ limit of the XXX $x_0$; it is, to $10^{-31}$ (norms $\sim h$ per site).

The twist of the bra vacuum is conjugated into the diagonal frame, $g'=P_{\rm aux}(\zeta)\,g_c(\omega)\,P_{\rm aux}(\zeta)^{-1}$, where $\omega=(\omega_1,\omega_2,\omega_3)$ are the three eigenvalues of the vacuum's own twist. The bra vacuum is
$$
\Omega_{\zeta\omega}=e_1^T\rho(n),\qquad n=\exp(r_1E_{12})\exp(r_2E_{13})\exp(r_3E_{23}),\label{eq:vac}
$$
$$
r_1=\frac{\omega_1-\zeta_1}{\omega_1-\zeta_2},\quad r_2=\frac{(\omega_1-\zeta_1)(\zeta_1-\omega_2)(\zeta_2-\zeta_3)}{(\zeta_1-\zeta_2)(\omega_1-\zeta_3)(\omega_2-\zeta_3)},\quad r_3=\frac{(\omega_1-\zeta_2)(\zeta_2-\omega_2)(\zeta_1-\zeta_3)}{(\zeta_1-\zeta_2)(\omega_1-\zeta_3)(\zeta_3-\omega_2)}.\label{eq:rk}
$$
We verified that $\Omega_{\zeta\omega}\otimes\Omega_{\zeta\omega}$ is an exact left eigencovector of all coefficient operators of $S_2,S_3$ built with the twist $g'$, for $[2,1,0]$, $[3,1,0]$, $[3,2,0]$ at three twist points; the four other orientations of the conjugation, the transposes, and the naive control $\mathrm{diag}(\omega)$ all fail.^[Agents P1, P2 (independent), W1; `RESULTS-Qside.md` section 0, `RESULTS-xxx-limit.md` X1, `Gaudin-L2-chain.wb`. The $r_k$ are the same degree-zero homogeneous functions as in XXX: $r_k(z,w)\to r_k(\zeta,\omega)$ with $O(h)$ corrections, and that is why the Gaudin vacuum is \emph{exactly} $\epsilon$-independent in the regime $\zeta=\epsilon\phi$, $\omega=\epsilon\hat\omega$. At $\zeta=(3/2,5/4,2/3)$, $\omega=(5/2,7/3,3/5)$ the Gauss parameters are $r=(4/5,-7/11,65/44)$, rep-independent.] The covector $\Omega_{\zeta\omega}$ is the vacuum of a \emph{different} twist than the ket's; its overlaps with the $\zeta$-eigenstates are the objects the SoV machinery turns into Q-functions.

At $\omega_2\to\infty$ the vacuum has a finite limit $\Omega^\infty[\zeta,\omega_1]=e_1^T\rho(n^\infty)$ with
$$
r_1=\frac{\omega_1-\zeta_1}{\omega_1-\zeta_2},\quad r_2=-\frac{(\omega_1-\zeta_1)(\zeta_2-\zeta_3)}{(\zeta_1-\zeta_2)(\omega_1-\zeta_3)},\quad r_3=\frac{(\omega_1-\zeta_2)(\zeta_1-\zeta_3)}{(\zeta_1-\zeta_2)(\omega_1-\zeta_3)},\label{eq:rkinf}
$$
and it depends on only two parameters in the two-site family $\Phi_\infty=\Omega^\infty[\zeta,\omega_1]\otimes\Omega^\infty[\zeta,\omega_1']$. In the companion frame the same vacuum has Gauss parameters $(1/\omega_1,\,-1/(\omega_1\omega_2),\,(\omega_1+\omega_2)/(\omega_1\omega_2))$, i.e. exactly the XXX ones with $w\to\omega$, and at $\omega_2=\infty$ it is $\Omega_c^\infty[\omega_1]=e_1^T\rho(e^{aE_{12}}e^{aE_{23}})$ with $a=1/\omega_1$. The two frames are tied by
$$
\Omega_D[\zeta,\omega]\,P^G_{\rm phys}(\zeta)=\kappa^G(\zeta,\omega)\,\Omega_c[\omega],\qquad \kappa^G=\Delta_1^{\lambda_1-\lambda_2}\Delta_2^{\lambda_2-\lambda_3}\Delta_3^{\lambda_3},\label{eq:kappaG}
$$
with $\Delta_1=\omega_1^2/((\zeta_2-\omega_1)(\zeta_3-\omega_1))$ and, at $\omega_2=\infty$, $\Delta_2=\omega_1/((\zeta_1-\zeta_2)(\zeta_3-\omega_1))$. This gives the scalar that appears throughout the kernel normalisation,
$$
c=\frac{(\omega_1\omega_1')^{2\lambda_1-\lambda_2}}{\kappa^G(\zeta,\omega_1)\,\kappa^G(\zeta,\omega_1')}=(\zeta_2-\omega_1)^{\lambda_1-\lambda_2}(\zeta_3-\omega_1)^{\lambda_1}(\zeta_2-\omega_1')^{\lambda_1-\lambda_2}(\zeta_3-\omega_1')^{\lambda_1}(\zeta_1-\zeta_2)^{2\lambda_2}.\label{eq:cfactor}
$$
It was derived for the adjoint ($c=(\zeta_2-\omega_1)(\zeta_3-\omega_1)^2(\ldots)(\zeta_1-\zeta_2)^2$), $[3,1,0]$ and $[3,2,0]$, with the other exponent choices excluded by the fits.^[Agents W2/W3 (frame tie, `Gaudin-L2-chain-2.wb` task A, `Gaudin-L2-chain-320.wb` A2) and P4 (`RESULTS-Qside-320.md` T0): with the correct $c$ the per-$\zeta$ fits reproduce the stacked kernel to $10^{-71}$ to $10^{-74}$; the wrong exponents $(1,2,2),(1,3,1),(0,3,2),(2,3,2),(1,3,3),(1,4,2)$ give residuals $0.18$ to $0.69$.]

## The XXX scaffold in one paragraph {#sec:scaffold}

All of the above was first understood in the XXX chain with $L=2$ sites in the new conventions (Lax $u\delta_{ij}+hE_{ij}$, companion twist on the right, $B$ and $C$ built from the untwisted $T$). There, the SoV basis consists of the eigencovectors of $B(u)$, with simple spectrum and labels $(a|bc)$ per site, and the overlap of the vacuum $\Omega_{zw}=e_1^T\rho(n(z,w))$ with an eigenstate is a determinant or a sum of products of ladder brackets of the Q-functions. For the family of two rotated vacua $\Omega_w\otimes\Omega_{w'}$ the untwisting $z,w,w'\to1$ was clean: $K/S=O(\epsilon)$ on every HW branch, all CG data at leading order, and for the adjoint $(\infty,\infty)$ family the Q-number is a sum of ten products of $2\times2$ bracket determinants.^[`Paul/Mathematica/new-conventions/Two-site-rotation-brackets-notes.md`, sections 0 and 5 (Stages D and C), notebooks `L2-two-vacua-untwist.wb` and `L2-210-two-vacua-brackets.wb`; the XXX Q-number reproduces the chain overlap to $7.9\cdot10^{-111}$ and the six adjoint CG data to $10^{-18}$.] The XXX program also needed, for the single-vacuum approach, a "cleaning" step for some sectors (`Clean-CG-combinations.md`). The Gaudin model is the $h\to0$ limit, equivalently $\theta_{12}/h\to\infty$ at fixed twist, because of the scaling symmetry $(u-\theta)\to(u-\theta)/\lambda$, $g\to\lambda g$. The XXX companion matrix is not the exponential of a Lie-algebra companion matrix (at $z=1$ it is a Jordan block), which is why the Gaudin companion frame had to be defined separately; the Jordan block turned out to be harmless, since $(G'-1)/h\to g'$ at $O(h)$.

## Representations, bases and numerical points {#sec:points}

We label the GT basis of $V_\Lambda$ in \emph{notebook order}: index $1$ is the highest weight, patterns sorted in descending order. The Python modules use the \emph{`gl3_reps` order}, which starts at the lowest-weight vector, so that the HW ket has the last index ($7$ for the adjoint, $14$ for $[3,1,0]$ and $[3,2,0]$). The map is a permutation, not a reversal: for the adjoint, notebook index $a=0,\dots,7$ corresponds to `gl3_reps` index $(7,5,6,4,2,1,3,0)$.^[Agent D3, `RESULTS-gaudin-B.md` header (`NB2G`), also D1 `RESULTS-twovac-fit.md` section 0 (the $x_0^G$ direction of P2 mapped with `NB2G` is reproduced to $10^{-81}$). Agent W4's data files use 1-based notebook order; P1/P3/P4, D1, U2 use `gl3_reps` order. In this text, slices and indices are in notebook order unless said otherwise.] The two-site product basis has index $(i,j)\mapsto(i-1)\dim V+j$, site 1 slow, site 2 fast.

The numerical points reused across all agents are these. Inhomogeneities $\theta=(2/7,-3/5)$, $\theta_{12}=31/35$. Twist points $P:\ \zeta=(3/2,5/4,2/3)$ and $P':\ \zeta=(2,5/3,-1/2)$, with extra points $(7/5,-3/4,1/6)$, $(-4/3,2/9,5/7)$, $(1/3,-2/7,5/11)$. Vacuum twists $\omega=(5/2,7/3,3/5)$, $\omega'=(7/4,-2/5,9/7)$ and the two-parameter pair $(\omega_1,\omega_1')=(5/2,7/4)$. The untwisting regime is $\zeta=\epsilon\phi$, $\omega=\epsilon\hat\omega$, $\omega'=\epsilon\hat\omega'$ with $\phi=(1/3,-2/7,5/11)$ (control direction $\phi'=(-2/5,3/8,7/11)$), and $\epsilon\in\{1,2,4\}\cdot10^{-6}$ for the chain-side checks and $\epsilon_k=10^{-6}2^{-k}$, $k=0,\dots,11$, for the series fits; precision is 200 digits on the chain side and 60 to 120 digits on the Q side. The full list of grids is in Appendix~\ref{app:conv}.
